%U A120221 3,2,1,3,4,2,1,3,1,3,1,1,1,2,2,2,1,2,1,5,3,3,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,3,2,1,2,1,2,2,3
%N A120221 Continued fraction expansion of one minus the Minkowski-Bower constant.
%C A120221 Continued fraction expansions for the other fixed points (mod 1) are {0}, A058914 and {0,2}.
%C A120221 It is conjectured that Minkowski's question mark function has exactly five fixed points on [0,1], or four modulo 1; see Gayfulin and Shulga, Conjecture 1. - _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 08 2026
%H A120221 D. Gayfulin and N. A. Shulga, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.10139">Diophantine properties of fixed points of Minkowski question mark function</a>, arXiv:1811.10139 [math.NT], 2018-2019; Acta Arithmetica 195 (2020), 367-382. See Conjecture 1.
%H A120221 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Minkowski-BowerConstant.html">Minkowski-Bower Constant</a>.
%H A120221 <a href="/index/Me#MinkowskiQ">Index entries for Minkowski's question mark function</a>
%H A120221 <a href="/index/Me#MinkowskiQ">Index entries for sequences related to Minkowski's question mark function</a>
%t A120221 ContinuedFraction[1 - (x /. FindRoot[MinkowskiQuestionMark[x] == x, {x, 42/100, 421/1000}, WorkingPrecision -> 100, MaxIterations -> 500]), 20] (* _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 08 2026 *)
%Y A120221 Cf. A058914.
%Y A120221 Cf. A048819 (decimal expansion of the Minkowski-Bower constant).
%Y A120221 Cf. A048820 (binary expansion of the Minkowski-Bower constant).
%K A120221 cofr,nonn,changed
